-- title:
--   Invertibility descends from an open chart containing the support
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ and $X$ be schemes, let $j\colon W \to X$ be an open immersion, and let $I$ be a quasi-coherent ideal sheaf on $X$, presented as an `IdealSheafData`, i.e. by the assignment of an ideal $I(U) \subseteq \Gamma(X, U)$ to each affine open $U$ of $X$ compatible with restriction to basic opens. Assume that the support of $I$, as a subset of the underlying space of $X$, is contained in the image $\operatorname{range}(j)$, and that the inverse image ideal sheaf $I.\mathrm{comap}\ j$ on $W$ satisfies the predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $w$ of $W$ there are an affine open $U$ of $W$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(W, U)$ with $w \in W.\mathrm{basicOpen}\ f$, together with a non-zero-divisor $g$ in $\Gamma(W, W.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\ f)$ such that the ideal attached to the affine open $W.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\ f$ is the principal ideal $(g)$. The conclusion is that $I$ itself satisfies `IsInvertible` in exactly this sense on $X$: each point of $X$ lies in a basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\ f$ of an affine open $U \subseteq X$ on whose associated affine basic open the ideal of $I$ is generated by a single non-zero-divisor.
--
--   This is the statement that invertibility of an ideal sheaf, in the local form 'generated by one non-zero-divisor on affine basic opens', is a local property that may be checked on any open subscheme whose image contains the support. It is used in the treatment of relative effective Cartier divisors on curves and of the relative Picard functor, where invertibility of the ideal of a section, or of a power of it, is first established on an affine chart.
-- source:
